neutron-zzzzzzzzzg-winewayland-wheel-notch-accumulator

winewayland: optionally snap fragmented high-resolution wheel input to whole notches
(NEUTRON_WHEEL_NOTCH=1, off by default).

SYMPTOM (Adobe Premiere Pro, user-reported): one physical ratchet click of a Logitech
MX Master 2S scrubbed the timeline a DIFFERENT distance every time -- measured at 3 to 8
frames per detent -- where Windows moves exactly one.

CAUSE, measured, not inferred. A high-resolution wheel (HID++ "HIRES WHEEL" {2121},
multiplier 8) does not deliver one value120=120 per detent. Captured with a purpose-built
meter (preserved-fixes/harnesses/wheelmeter.c) plus WINEDEBUG=+waylanddrv, five deliberate
clicks per run, value120 histogram:

    hires ON  :  61 x 15,  13 x 60,  2 x 30,  1 x 75
    hires OFF :  32 x 15,   7 x 60

One detent arrives as an IRREGULAR burst, because the layers below coalesce fragments by
TIMING rather than by detent. (Note the "OFF" column: Solaar's hires-smooth-resolution
toggle did not reach the event stream at all -- the device stayed in multiplier-8 mode.)

Wine forwards each fragment as its own WM_MOUSEWHEEL, which is faithful to the protocol and
harmless for an app that sums deltas. An app that treats each message as one discrete step --
Premiere's timeline does -- therefore moves a different distance for the same physical click,
and no fixed scale factor can correct it because the fragmentation is non-deterministic.

FIX: accumulate value120 per axis and emit one WHEEL_DELTA per whole notch, carrying the
remainder. Deterministic (arithmetic, not timing) and reproduces exactly what a plain wheel
delivers on Windows: measured 1 message / +120 per detent, every detent. A direction change
abandons the partial notch rather than cancelling against it, so reversing never swallows the
first click back.

ENV-GATED AND OFF BY DEFAULT, because it is a compatibility choice rather than a conformance
fix: Windows with a high-resolution mouse also delivers fragments, and an app that sums deltas
wants them. The launcher enables it per-app for Adobe profiles.

⛔ REJECTED ALTERNATIVE, recorded so it is not retried: bounding the wineserver's
merge_mousewheel() to a single notch (server/queue.c). It looked right -- that merge is
unbounded and Windows does not coalesce wheel messages at all, so bursts of distinct notches
were collapsing into one message and an app counting messages lost detents. But with this
accumulator in place, fragments never reach the queue as separate messages, so the bound has
no defect left to fix -- while its remaining effect is to forbid ALL coalescing, which removes
the backpressure relief that keeps a busy app responsive. Tested live: the timeline became
"incredibly laggy, accumulates and is impossible to interrupt". Reverted; the server merge is
left stock.

Write-up: docs/wiki/TODO.md P1f.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c
@@ -368,6 +368,53 @@
 {
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: snap fragmented wheel input to whole notches (NEUTRON_WHEEL_NOTCH=1).
+ *
+ * A high-resolution wheel (Logitech HID++ "HIRES WHEEL", multiplier 8) does not deliver one
+ * value120=120 per physical detent. MEASURED on an MX Master 2S under KWin, one detent arrives
+ * as an IRREGULAR burst -- 15,15,15,15,15,15,15,15 or 60,15,15,15,15 or 60,60, and occasionally
+ * 30 or 75 -- because the layers below coalesce fragments by timing, not by detent. Wine forwards
+ * each fragment as its own WM_MOUSEWHEEL, which is faithful, but it means an app that treats each
+ * message as one discrete step (Premiere Pro's timeline does) moves a DIFFERENT distance for the
+ * same physical click every time: 3 to 8 frames per detent, measured.
+ *
+ * Accumulating and emitting per whole WHEEL_DELTA makes it deterministic -- arithmetic, not
+ * timing -- and reproduces exactly what a plain wheel delivers on Windows. Sub-notch precision is
+ * preserved as a remainder, so a slow deliberate turn still lands every notch.
+ *
+ * Env-gated because it IS a compatibility choice, not a conformance fix: Windows with a hi-res
+ * mouse also delivers fragments, and an app that sums deltas wants them. Enabled per-app by the
+ * launcher (see APP_PROFILES in bin/neutron). Not for touchpads: axis_source is checked by the
+ * caller only for wheels, and continuous scroll never reaches this handler on this driver. */
+static BOOL neutron_wheel_notch(void)
+{
+    static int v = -1;
+    if (v < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_WHEEL_NOTCH"); v = (e && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
+    return v;
+}
+
+static int32_t neutron_wheel_accumulate(uint32_t axis, int32_t value120)
+{
+    /* wl_pointer events are delivered on the single wayland event thread, so plain statics are
+     * safe here and match how the rest of this file keeps per-seat state. */
+    static int32_t accum[2];
+    int32_t *a, out;
+
+    if (axis != WL_POINTER_AXIS_VERTICAL_SCROLL &&
+        axis != WL_POINTER_AXIS_HORIZONTAL_SCROLL)
+        return value120;
+    a = &accum[axis == WL_POINTER_AXIS_HORIZONTAL_SCROLL];
+
+    /* A direction change abandons the partial notch rather than cancelling against it: the user
+     * physically reversed, and carrying a negative remainder would swallow the first click back. */
+    if ((*a < 0) != (value120 < 0)) *a = 0;
+
+    *a += value120;
+    out = (*a / 120) * 120;
+    *a -= out;
+    return out;
+}
+
 static void pointer_handle_axis_value120(void *data, struct wl_pointer *wl_pointer,
                                          uint32_t axis, int32_t value120)
 {
@@ -376,6 +423,12 @@
 
     if (!(hwnd = wayland_pointer_get_focused_hwnd())) return;
 
+    if (neutron_wheel_notch() && !(value120 = neutron_wheel_accumulate(axis, value120)))
+    {
+        TRACE("hwnd=%p axis=%u accumulating, no whole notch yet\n", hwnd, axis);
+        return;
+    }
+
     input.type = INPUT_MOUSE;
 
     switch (axis)
